The human pushed two new instructions in the middle of this round (11/08 01:45 and 01:40 KST), so most of the round went to them. Everything is committed and pushed, and local main matches r2e/main.

**New human instructions**
- **Final method:** MoTT+P1 plus up to 4 rounds of verifier feedback (`--repair 4`), named `MoTT+P1+V`. No more training-side candidates. The CPU queue now runs the F1 → F2 → F3 repair evals.
- **Cancelled evals:** the human moved the remaining 11 `r2pkfc_s2` 16/20-cell evals to `cpu_deferred` and killed the one that was running (the rc=-15 at 16:33). I did not restart them.
- **Server 2's share:** it runs F1 pkg20 (r2pk, 3 seeds) and F2, and pushes each record as it finishes.

**What I did**
- **Merge helper:** the driver only checks for result files on disk and never pulls, so it would re-run server 2's items. A new background helper, `scripts/pull_s2_records.py`, merges server 2's pushes every 3 minutes. It skips the merge if an incoming file already exists here. 7 of 18 records have merged so far, with no conflicts.
- **Memory problem with side_slot:** I first let side_slot run the two coordinate pkg20 evals (not server 2's) next to the driver's F1 pkg16. That took the pod to **45.7 GiB**: the pkg16 repair worker alone is 22 GB, and past pkg16 repair runs peaked at 25–28 GB. The human allowed this only below 45 GiB. So I stopped the side job (it had written no record yet) and took F back out of side_slot. Pod is now 26 GiB, and big-board repair evals run one at a time in the driver.
- **Results table:** `collect_results.py` has a new "최종 방법 MoTT+P1+V" table: size × kind × seed, policy-alone vs with feedback, part-count changes, and paired CIs against coordinate+V and greedy+prune+V. It shows only numbers; the human applies readings (a)(b)(c). It appears once the first F record lands. I checked its layout with a dummy record.

